---
import TutorialLayout from '../../../layouts/TutorialLayout.astro';
import ModelWorkbench from '../../../components/tutorials/ModelWorkbench';
---

<TutorialLayout
  title="Your First Model"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { label: 'Tutorials', href: '/tutorials' },
    { label: 'Getting Started', href: '/tutorials' },
    { label: 'Your First Model' },
  ]}
  next={{ label: 'Mass-Spring-Damper', href: '/tutorials/getting-started/mass-spring-damper' }}
>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    Welcome to Rumoca. In this tutorial you will write a Modelica model, compile it, and simulate it
    &mdash; all in your browser. No install required.
  </p>

  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    We will start with the <strong class="text-text">double integrator</strong> &mdash; the simplest second-order
    ODE. A constant acceleration <em>a</em> drives velocity <em>v</em>, which drives position <em>x</em>:
  </p>

  <div class="p-4 rounded-lg bg-surface border border-border font-mono text-sm text-text-muted mb-6">
    dx/dt = v<br/>
    dv/dt = a
  </div>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mb-3">Try it</h2>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    The editor below contains a complete Modelica model. Click <strong class="text-text">Compile</strong> to
    parse it, then <strong class="text-text">Simulate</strong> to run 5 seconds of integration. Toggle variables
    in the plot legend to explore the results.
  </p>

  <ModelWorkbench
    client:only="react"
    initialSource={`model DoubleIntegrator "Double integrator (constant acceleration)"
  parameter Real a = 1.0 "Constant acceleration (m/s^2)";
  Real x(start = 0.0) "Position (m)";
  Real v(start = 0.0) "Velocity (m/s)";
equation
  der(x) = v;
  der(v) = a;
end DoubleIntegrator;`}
    modelName="DoubleIntegrator"
    tEnd={5}
    defaultScript={`ctx.onInit = (api) => {
  const { THREE, state } = api;
  state.scene.background = new THREE.Color(0x101010);
  const light = new THREE.DirectionalLight(0xffffff, 1.0);
  light.position.set(2, 4, 3);
  state.scene.add(light);
  state.scene.add(new THREE.AmbientLight(0x404040, 0.9));
  state.scene.add(new THREE.GridHelper(12, 24, 0x2f4f63, 0x2a2a2a));
  // Floor
  const floor = new THREE.Mesh(
    new THREE.PlaneGeometry(14, 14),
    new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x1a1a2e, side: THREE.DoubleSide })
  );
  floor.rotation.x = -Math.PI / 2;
  floor.position.y = -0.01;
  state.scene.add(floor);
  // Sphere
  state.sphere = new THREE.Mesh(
    new THREE.SphereGeometry(0.2, 32, 24),
    new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x38bdf8 })
  );
  state.sphere.position.y = 0.2;
  state.scene.add(state.sphere);
  // Trail
  state.trailPoints = [];
  state.trail = new THREE.Line(
    new THREE.BufferGeometry(),
    new THREE.LineBasicMaterial({ color: 0x38bdf8, transparent: true, opacity: 0.5 })
  );
  state.scene.add(state.trail);
};
ctx.onFrame = (api) => {
  const x = api.getValue("x", api.sampleIndex);
  const pos = Number.isFinite(x) ? x * 0.4 : 0;
  if (api.state.sphere) {
    api.state.sphere.position.x = pos;
    api.state.trailPoints.push(new api.THREE.Vector3(pos, 0.2, 0));
    if (api.state.trailPoints.length > 500) api.state.trailPoints.shift();
    api.state.trail.geometry.dispose();
    api.state.trail.geometry = new api.THREE.BufferGeometry().setFromPoints(api.state.trailPoints);
  }
};`}
  />

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mt-10 mb-3">What just happened?</h2>
  <ol class="list-decimal list-inside text-text-muted leading-relaxed space-y-2 mb-6">
    <li><strong class="text-text">Compile</strong> &mdash; Rumoca parsed the Modelica source and produced a DAE intermediate representation (try switching the backend dropdown to see different output formats).</li>
    <li><strong class="text-text">Simulate</strong> &mdash; The ODE was integrated in your browser using WebAssembly. No server round-trip.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-text">Plot</strong> &mdash; State trajectories are plotted over time. Position <em>x</em> grows quadratically; velocity <em>v</em> grows linearly.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-text">3D Viewer</strong> &mdash; Switch to the 3D tab to see the sphere accelerating along the floor.</li>
  </ol>

  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    This is the core Rumoca workflow: <strong class="text-text">write &rarr; compile &rarr; simulate &rarr; visualize</strong>.
    Every tutorial that follows builds on this foundation with more complex models and additional features
    like CasADi code generation, symbolic analysis, and 3D visualization.
  </p>
</TutorialLayout>
